WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:05.320
We have seen parts of a function and how they&nbsp;
work. Let’s put these parts together and define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:11.560
our own function. As we define a function, pay&nbsp;
close attention to the keyword used to define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:18.000
a function, as it’s not the word, function, as&nbsp;
is the case in many other languages. To start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.080
I have a blank file, except for a comment on line&nbsp;
1 explaining the function we are about to build,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:30.440
which will multiply a score and multiplier&nbsp;
and return the result. The keyword to define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:38.280
a function is def, short for definition. I&nbsp;
will type that keyword, def, on line 2. Next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:44.360
we need a name for the function. I will name&nbsp;
this calculate_score, with an underscore between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:52.800
the two words. Next, we need to determine if the&nbsp;
function will take arguments, variables that will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:59.960
be used in a condition, calculation, or both.&nbsp;
Since this function will multiply two numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:07.640
I will add two arguments in parentheses: score and&nbsp;
multiplier. Then, as with many other code blocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.920
I will add a colon to the end of the line.&nbsp;
Most functions will want to return a value to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.400
be used later in code. As we saw in the previous&nbsp;
topic, the return keyword returns a value that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:25.280
can be used later in code. Any code running&nbsp;
within a function must be indented under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:33.160
def statement. For this example, we only need one&nbsp;
line to return the score times the multiplier. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:41.800
call this function, I will type, on line 5, the&nbsp;
name of the function, and then, in parentheses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:56.320
the score and the multiplier, wrapped inside&nbsp;
of a print statement. I will run this code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:02.760
and we will see the result. Next, let’s see&nbsp;
the main benefit of functions. I will copy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:15.800
the print statements calling the function,&nbsp;
change the numbers, and rerun the code. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:21.840
same block of code is now run twice. One could&nbsp;
run a function thousands of times if needed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.800
and if the function changes, the code for&nbsp;
the function only needs to be changed once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:33.080
thus increasing the efficiency of code&nbsp;
and helping to prevent errors in code.

